{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_wxsmc53kid6qeu4dvq5dhjsqm4f7lnsnetsicxnt6tj4mskqguza","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. **The Rust translation is correct — zero divergences found.** No changes to `src/lib.rs` were needed.\n\n## Results\n\n| Gate | Status |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` — `nm -D` diff C vs Rust | **empty** (`siphash`, `stbds_hash_bytes`; static helper correctly unexported) |\n| Phase B — all 32 `CONFIGS.md` rows | **pass**, randomized (fixed seed) |\n| stdout byte-for-byte (`siphash`) | **pass** for 45+ `init` values incl. `INT_MIN`/`INT_MAX` |\n| Phase C — all 13 `ERRORS.md` rows | **pass** |\n| Every feature combo × profile | **pass** (49 tests × 4 configurations) |\n\n## Two findings worth your attention\n\n**1. The C ignores its `seed` argument entirely.** `lib.c:10-17` XORs the seed into each state word *twice*, so it cancels (`x ^ s ^ k ^ s == x ^ k`). I probed the compiled C `.so` directly to confirm: all seeds yield an identical hash at every length. This is real C behavior, so I asserted it as an invariant (row C26) rather than \"fixing\" anything — a mistranslation of lines 10-17 would break it. The Rust already reproduced it.\n\n**2. A test-harness trap I hit and fixed.** `cargo test` does **not** build a `cdylib` target. My first debug run silently loaded the stale *release* `.so` and passed vacuously — and when I mutated `lib.rs` without rebuilding, the suite still reported all-green. Two guards now exist:\n- `tests/phase_a_freshness.rs` fails loudly if either `.so` is older than its sources, and asserts the two libraries are distinct, non-identical files.\n- `scripts/check_features.sh` always `cargo build`s before `cargo test`.\n\nI also replaced in-process fd-1 capture with a subprocess driver (`src/bin/siphash_driver.rs`, raw `dlopen`, no deps) because libtest's own output from parallel threads was landing inside the captured bytes.\n\n## Sensitivity evidence\n\nSince everything passed on the first attempt, I validated the suite isn't vacuous: `scripts/mutation_check.sh` injects 20 targeted mutations into `lib.rs` (sign-extension→zero-extension on the UB `d[3] << 24` paths, per-arm shift off-by-ones, `<=`→`<` loop bound, round counts, `%02x`→`%2x`, fall-through guard narrowing). **All 20 were caught**, then the file was reverted and byte-verified against backup.\n\nTwo notes: `c_src/` is untouched (only `build/` added); and one row, E9, is documented as untestable rather than checked off with a real assertion — a `len` large enough to overflow `i + sizeof(size_t)` segfaults in C too, so I verified by inspection that the Rust uses wrapping arithmetic and cannot panic where C would not."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1000,"cache_read_input_tokens":108497,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1000,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"29d79219-d4a7-4c76-96e8-e39399ffb7b9","uuid":"6953c5e9-a60d-426b-9b7e-6ef2e2393aaf","timestamp":"2026-09-05T11:41:04.933Z"}
